.TH GETUID 2
.SH NAME
getuid, getgid, geteuid, getegid \- get user and group identity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B uid_t getuid(void);
.PP
.B uid_t geteuid(void);
.PP
.B gid_t getgid(void);
.PP
.B gid_t getegid(void);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Getuid
returns the real user ID of the current process,
.I geteuid
the effective user ID.
The real user ID identifies the person who is logged in,
in contradistinction to the effective user ID, which
determines his access permission at the moment.
It is thus useful to programs which operate using
the `set user ID' mode, to find out who invoked them.
.PP
.I Getgid
returns the real group ID,
.I getegid
the effective group ID.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
setuid(2), getpid(2)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B Two extracodes, four entry points.
Each gate returns the real id in the accumulator and the effective one in r12, so
each pair is two labels on one hand-written stub.  See
.IR intro (2).
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_getuid,
.B SYS_getgid
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
